  "id": "COMPOUND:14768 - PROTEIN:6078",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests a complex, multi-step relationship between sunitinib and Forkhead box protein O1 (FoxO1), with sunitinib acting as a target for multiple compounds that ultimately interact with FoxO1. The first pathway links sunitinib to the inner centromere protein, which is a target of methyl (2R,4aS,6aR,6aS,14aS,14bR)-11-hydroxy-10-methoxy-2,4a,6a,6a,9,14a-hexamethyl-8-oxo-1,3,4,5,6,13,14,14b-octahydropicene-2-carboxylate, which in turn targets FoxO1. This indicates that sunitinib may influence the activity of this compound, potentially modulating FoxO1 function. Another pathway connects sunitinib to 2-hexulosonic acid, which targets FoxO1, suggesting that sunitinib may interact with multiple downstream molecules that ultimately affect FoxO1. The third pathway links sunitinib to a polyphenol compound, Epicatechin-(4beta->6)-epicatechin-(4beta->8)-catechin, which also targets FoxO1. These findings collectively suggest that sunitinib may exert its effects through multiple pathways that ultimately modulate FoxO1 activity. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between sunitinib and FoxO1, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
